  "subtitle": "A push-button voting machine with a motor-driven paper scroll ballot display — democracy as a mechanical interface",
  "why_scout_cares": "The Microvote MV-464 is a dedicated-purpose DRE (direct-recording electronic) voting machine that embodies a unique interaction model: voters read ballot choices through a glass window from a motor-driven paper scroll, and press physical buttons on either side to make selections. The machine folds into a voting booth. It is a complete, self-contained HCI artifact where the interface is inseparable from the civic function. No other artifact in the museum captures the 'machine as democratic instrument' paradigm — the collision of mechanical engineering, civic ritual, and human-computer interaction. The paper scroll behind glass is a physical ballot rendered as a mechanical display, prefiguring the touchscreen voting machines of the 2000s while remaining tethered to paper-based election processes.",
